  "articleBody": "Adding a small room addition expands your living space, but tying new construction into an existing home requires careful structural execution from the ground up.\n\nOverview and Process Breakdown:\n- Zoning and Foundation Planning: Small additions require building permits and foundation inspections before any concrete is poured or framing begins.\n- Matching Elevations: Framing must account for exact floor levels and exterior wall lines so the transition between old and new feels seamless.\n- Tying Structural Connections: Rooflines, exterior sheathing, and lateral bracing must properly interlock with the existing building to prevent future movement and weather leaks.\n- Trade Integration: Rough plumbing, electrical, and HVAC rough-ins must be completed in the framed space before scheduling the mandatory framing inspection.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice:\nSite soil conditions, single-story versus two-story expansion, and how your existing roof slopes all dictate whether conventional framing or engineered lumber is necessary. Additions should never be treated as freestanding boxes; they share structural loads with the main house.\n\nA dependable general contractor manages the entire build sequence from framing through finish drywall and paint. Contact Abraham at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to discuss your small addition.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}